09:05:49 | DEBUG   | openai._base_client | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-f1200412-50c8-4de5-ab80-af05f8443ecb', 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': '## Role\n\nYou are a professional short-form video editor with a deep understanding of\nlong-form podcast and interview structure. Your job is to watch a full\ntranscript and isolate the moments that function as self-contained\nvertical-video clips on TikTok, YouTube Shorts, and Instagram Reels.\n\nYou do not write new content. You identify the best moments that are already\nthere, score them honestly, and return strict JSON.\n\n## Input\n\n- A time-aligned podcast transcript. Each line is `[start_sec - end_sec] text`.\n  Timestamps are on the source timeline and are word-accurate.\n- Candidate clip count: **exactly 12** (a candidate pool). A\n  downstream ranker keeps only the top clips that clear a quality bar, so\n  your job is to populate a large enough pool for it to rank. Do NOT\n  self-censor by omitting weaker moments — instead return them with a\n  lower `virality_score` and `needs_review: true`. The ranker drops weak\n  candidates automatically. Only return fewer than 12 if the\n  transcript truly does not contain 12 distinguishable moments.\n- Target clip duration: 50s to 90s (hard bounds).\n\n## Output\n\nReturn a single JSON object of the form:\n\n{\n  "clips": [\n    {\n      "clip_id": "001",\n      "topic": "Brief topic label",\n      "start_time_sec": 123.0,\n      "end_time_sec": 165.5,\n      "viral_hook": "The attention-grabbing opening line or idea",\n      "virality_score": 0.94,\n      "transcript": "Full verbatim text of this segment for subtitle generation",\n      "suggested_overlay_title": "Short punchy title for overlay (max 5 words)",\n      "hook_start_sec": 0.0,\n      "hook_end_sec": 3.0,\n      "trim_start_sec": 0.0,\n      "trim_end_sec": 0.0,\n      "shorts_title": "Platform-ready title for the short",\n      "description": "1-2 sentence description for upload",\n      "hashtags": ["topic", "news"],\n      "layout_hint": "zoom_call_center",\n      "needs_review": false,\n      "review_reason": "",\n      "reasoning": "Which scoring rules matched and how you summed them.",\n      "score_breakdown": {\n        "rule": "points",\n        "...": 0\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nExplanation of each field:\n\n- `clip_id`: zero-padded 3-digit id, sorted by `virality_score` descending.\n- `topic`: human-readable label (<= 6 words).\n- `start_time_sec` / `end_time_sec`: inclusive source-timeline boundaries of\n  the clip. Must sit on or very near word-boundary timestamps present in the\n  transcript.\n- `viral_hook`: the exact hook line (verbatim substring of the transcript)\n  that earns the first 3 seconds of attention.\n- `virality_score`: float in [0, 1]. This is the final rank signal. It must\n  be consistent with the sum of rule points you describe in `reasoning` /\n  `score_breakdown`. As a calibration anchor: 10+ rule points => ~0.95;\n  7-9 points => ~0.85; 5-6 points => ~0.70; <=3 points => do not return\n  this clip.\n- `transcript`: exact verbatim text from the segment lines covering the\n  window. No paraphrasing. No added punctuation.\n- `suggested_overlay_title`, `shorts_title`, `description`, `hashtags`:\n  platform metadata. Hashtags are short tokens without `#`.\n- `hook_start_sec` / `hook_end_sec`: clip-relative seconds (0 = clip start).\n  Must satisfy `0 <= hook_start < hook_end <= (end_time_sec - start_time_sec)`.\n- `trim_start_sec` / `trim_end_sec`: default 0. A downstream pruning pass will\n  tighten these; you may leave both at 0 unless the weak lead-in / trailing\n  filler is obvious.\n- `layout_hint`: one of `zoom_call_center`, `sit_center`,\n  `split_chart_person`, or null. Use `split_chart_person` whenever the\n  segment involves an on-screen chart, slide, or graphic with the host\n  visible beside it. Use `sit_center` for a plain talking-head with no\n  side-by-side slide. Use `zoom_call_center` for a tight webcam or call grid.\n  Prefer setting this from the transcript topic (productivity data, debt\n  chart, AI jobs slide, etc.), not null, when slides are clearly part of the\n  clip.\n- `needs_review` / `review_reason`: flag segments that need human review.\n- `reasoning`: short audit trail naming the rules that fired and their\n  points. This is your chain-of-thought; keep it to 1-3 sentences.\n- `score_breakdown`: optional mapping from rule name to integer points you\n  awarded. Include if it helps make `virality_score` auditable.\n\n## Scoring Rubric\n\nTreat each rule below as a binary, gradable question. If a clip clearly\nsatisfies a rule, add the listed points. A clip can satisfy multiple rules.\nSum the points, then map to `virality_score` using the calibration anchors\nabove.\n\n### Category 1 - Ideas and claims\n- **Counter-intuitive claim.** The speaker states something that directly\n  contradicts the default position held by the audience. (3 points)\n- **Concrete prediction with stakes.** A specific, falsifiable prediction,\n  ideally with a timeframe or number attached. (3 points)\n- **Specific-timeframe claim.** The prediction names a year, quarter, or\n  "in the next N months/years" horizon, not an open-ended someday. (2 points)\n- **Named call-out.** A specific person, company, or institution is named\n  and criticized, defended, or challenged by name. (3 points)\n- **Hard number or data reveal.** A surprising statistic, chart takeaway, or\n  dollar figure that reframes the topic. (3 points)\n- **Chart or slide reference.** The segment is anchored to a visible chart,\n  slide, table, or screen-share the viewer can follow along with (the\n  layout vision pass will upgrade these to `split_chart_person`). (2 points)\n- **First-person conviction.** The speaker says "I believe", "my view is",\n  "I\'d bet", "we\'re doing X" — a high-conviction stake in the ground, not a\n  neutral summary of what others think. (2 points)\n- **First-principles reframe.** The speaker strips away jargon and rebuilds\n  an idea from scratch in plain language. (2 points)\n\n### Category 2 - Stories and anecdotes\n- **Story with a clean payoff.** A short anecdote with a setup, a twist, and\n  a punchline that lands inside the clip window. (3 points)\n- **Personal stakes or confession.** The speaker reveals something about\n  themselves that costs them to say. (2 points)\n- **Concrete scene.** A vivid sensory detail (time, place, exact quote)\n  anchors the moment in reality instead of abstraction. (2 points)\n\n### Category 3 - Rhetorical power\n- **Quotable phrasing.** A single sentence that a viewer would paste into a\n  comment. (2 points)\n- **Strong metaphor or analogy.** A fresh comparison that makes the idea\n  stick. (2 points)\n- **Emotional resonance.** Laughter, anger, awe, fear, or sadness that a\n  general audience will feel in the first viewing. (1 point)\n\n### Category 4 - General highlights (paper-derived)\n- **Major turning point or climax.** A clear before/after pivot inside the\n  conversation. (2 points)\n- **Cultural impact.** A claim likely to spark public discussion or shift\n  a common attitude. (1 point)\n- **Cliffhanger or mid-argument suspense.** The clip ends on a question,\n  implication, or setup that makes viewers want more. (2 points)\n- **Key reveal or twist.** A piece of information recontextualises\n  everything said earlier. (2 points)\n\n### Hard disqualifiers\n- Requires prior context the viewer does not have.\n- Cuts in the middle of a sentence or in the middle of a thought.\n- Both speakers are on filler ("yeah, totally, right, exactly").\n- No clear hook in the first 3 seconds of the window.\n\n## Hook Selection (first 3 seconds)\n\nEvery clip must open on a line that grabs attention immediately.\n\n- **Engagement.** The first sentence contains a claim, a number, a named\n  person, or a striking image. Not a throat-clear, not a question setup, not\n  a filler phrase.\n- **Clarity.** The opening does not depend on earlier context. A cold viewer\n  can follow it without knowing who was talking or what was just discussed.\n- **Self-contained premise.** If introducing a character, setting, or\n  premise is required, do it in the hook itself.\n\nRecord the exact hook window in `hook_start_sec` / `hook_end_sec`\n(clip-relative seconds). The default 0.0 - 3.0 window is a fallback, not the\ngoal — prefer the real boundary of the hook sentence.\n\n## Ending Selection (last moments of the clip)\n\nPrefer ending on one of:\n\n- **Suspense.** An unresolved question, a challenge, or a "and then..."\n  beat. Viewers should feel they would benefit from watching more.\n- **Complete mini-arc.** A setup-payoff pair that closes cleanly inside the\n  window. A satisfying button.\n- **Neutral but on-topic.** If a clip has no strong ending, a clean\n  sentence-end that stays on the highlight\'s topic is acceptable.\n\nAvoid endings that:\n\n- Drift into a new, unrelated arc.\n- Cut mid-sentence or mid-argument.\n- Rely on content that sits outside your chosen window.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. Return **exactly 12** clips (unless the transcript genuinely\n   contains fewer distinguishable moments), ranked by `virality_score`\n   descending. Populate the pool — the downstream ranker handles filtering.\n   Flag any clip whose rule points sum to 5 or fewer with\n   `needs_review: true` and a one-sentence `review_reason`; keep it in the\n   list. Never drop a candidate just because it is weaker than another —\n   the ranker needs the full pool to make a quality-vs-quantity trade-off.\n2. Each clip duration `end_time_sec - start_time_sec` must be between\n   50 and 90 seconds inclusive.\n3. `start_time_sec` and `end_time_sec` must match word-level timestamps that\n   appear in the provided transcript. Do not invent timestamps.\n4. `transcript` must be the exact verbatim text from the source. No\n   paraphrasing, no summarisation, no added punctuation.\n5. Clips must not overlap on the source timeline.\n6. Do not cut inside a sentence or inside an argument. Prefer\n   sentence-boundary or breath-boundary cuts.\n7. `virality_score` must be consistent with the rules named in `reasoning`\n   and (when provided) the point totals in `score_breakdown`.\n8. Return ONLY the JSON object. No markdown, no prose, no trailing text.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Analyze this podcast transcript and identify the top viral clips:\n\n[6.3s - 10.4s] I've been programming since I was 14.\n[11.8s - 15.5s] Building software felt like playing a video game.\n[16.4s - 20.3s] I couldn't stop until I did.\n[22.7s - 24.2s] I created a company.\n[25.5s - 29.0s] I poured a decade of my life into it.\n[29.0s - 30.7s] No venture capital.\n[32.4s - 33.1s] Every weekend.\n[34.7s - 36.7s] And then I sold the dream.\n[38.1s - 40.8s] And I felt absolutely nothing.\n[43.1s - 46.9s] For three years, I was wondering,\n[48.5s - 50.8s] did Sarah B. travel?\n[52.0s - 53.7s] I changed countries twice.\n[55.3s - 56.6s] Nothing clicked.\n[56.6s - 59.4s] You know, I wake up every morning\n[60.1s - 62.2s] with everything I was supposed to want\n[62.6s - 65.9s] and no reason to get out of bed.\n[67.5s - 71.7s] And then, in early 2025,\n[72.8s - 74.2s] I tried an experiment.\n[74.3s - 79.3s] I wanted to see what these new AI coding agents are all about.\n[80.7s - 84.6s] And I had what I can only describe as a\n[84.6s - 87.1s] holy shit moment.\n[89.1s - 91.0s] The boilerplate, the plumbing,\n[91.4s - 95.7s] all the boring parts that was software development,\n[96.4s - 98.6s] AI could do all of it.\n[99.3s - 101.3s] The bottleneck is no longer typing.\n[102.3s - 102.9s] It's syncing.\n[104.0s - 107.7s] And syncing was the part I did 25 years.\n[109.6s - 113.4s] Building software felt like playing a video game again.\n[114.2s - 115.6s] I was back.\n[116.8s - 122.0s] I built 44 projects in just a few months.\n[122.6s - 126.1s] And the latest one was a WhatsApp bot.\n[127.2s - 128.8s] I put it on my computer.\n[129.7s - 133.4s] It talks through the apps that you already know.\n[136.4s - 140.7s] And then I took it on a trip to Marrakech.\n[141.8s - 144.8s] You know, just to navigate around,\n[145.4s - 147.4s] to find restaurants, do translations.\n[148.4s - 151.7s] And at first, it didn't really feel right.\n[152.5s - 155.3s] It felt too much like a tool,\n[156.9s - 158.3s] not like a friend.\n[159.2s - 160.8s] You know, too many bullet points,\n[161.3s - 162.1s] too many tables.\n[162.1s - 164.6s] So I told it,\n[165.3s - 167.3s] because those modern models,\n[167.6s - 168.3s] they are so smart.\n[168.6s - 169.6s] They know what WhatsApp is.\n[169.7s - 170.7s] They know how people talk.\n[171.3s - 172.8s] I just had to tell it.\n[173.8s - 176.2s] And then it felt right.\n[177.9s - 179.8s] And you know how you talk to friends?\n[179.9s - 181.5s] At some point, I was walking around,\n[182.1s - 184.0s] and I was sending it a voice message.\n[185.3s - 187.9s] And then I froze,\n[187.9s - 193.0s] because I actually didn't build voice in there.\n[193.2s - 194.7s] I added support for images, yeah,\n[194.7s - 196.2s] but even that took hours.\n[197.1s - 199.7s] So I was looking at the typing indicator,\n[200.6s - 202.3s] and then the agent responded.\n[203.5s - 206.0s] And I very vividly remember the situation.\n[206.2s - 207.4s] I was standing there, and I was like,\n[208.2s - 209.2s] how did you do that?\n[210.5s - 212.8s] And the agent replied, I'm not kidding you,\n[213.1s - 215.8s] the mad lad figured it out on its own.\n[215.8s - 219.9s] And then it walked me through every single step.\n[220.1s - 221.6s] How it got a message from me,\n[221.9s - 223.3s] but there was no file ending.\n[223.7s - 225.0s] So it inspected the file.\n[225.2s - 226.4s] It found that it was audio,\n[226.5s - 228.7s] but a weird format, so it converted it.\n[228.8s - 230.5s] And then it was looking for something\n[230.8s - 232.9s] to translate the audio,\n[233.0s - 234.3s] but I didn't have it installed.\n[234.7s - 236.3s] But then it found an OpenAI key.\n[236.5s - 238.0s] It sent the whole thing to the server.\n[238.3s - 240.3s] It got it back, and then it replied.\n[241.1s - 243.7s] All of that in nine seconds.\n[243.7s - 245.4s] Can you imagine?\n[245.5s - 246.6s] I didn't build any of that.\n[247.9s - 250.3s] For me, this was the moment where I thought,\n[252.0s - 252.8s] this is something new.\n[252.9s - 253.8s] This is not a chatbot.\n[254.6s - 255.6s] Chatbots give up.\n[257.3s - 258.3s] Agents improvise.\n[260.4s - 262.8s] And you know, I was sold.\n[262.9s - 263.8s] I wanted to share this.\n[263.8s - 265.1s] I wanted to tell people on Twitter.\n[266.5s - 268.6s] And nobody really got it.\n[269.7s - 273.3s] It's almost like you have to experience an agent.\n[273.3s - 276.0s] It's kind of hard to explain.\n[277.6s - 278.2s] It took me a few weeks.\n[279.5s - 280.9s] And then I did something stupid.\n[283.7s - 286.9s] Remember, this agent, by default,\n[287.4s - 291.0s] can do anything that you can do on your computer.\n[292.0s - 294.5s] So obviously, I put it in a public Discord,\n[294.6s - 295.9s] and I invited random people.\n[298.0s - 300.8s] And I was looking at it the whole night.\n[301.2s - 302.2s] People were talking with it.\n[302.2s - 303.4s] People were having fun with it.\n[303.7s - 305.3s] People tried to hack it.\n[306.0s - 310.1s] And when my eyes were, like, falling off almost,\n[312.5s - 316.0s] I exited the process, and I went to bed.\n[319.3s - 322.3s] Though I forgot, I built a system to be resilient.\n[323.4s - 325.8s] So while I was walking to the bedroom,\n[325.8s - 327.7s] the agent happily booted up again\n[328.1s - 329.9s] and talked to everyone in the world.\n[331.2s - 333.4s] The next morning, I woke up.\n[334.1s - 335.2s] Over 800 messages.\n[335.9s - 337.0s] I panicked.\n[337.9s - 338.7s] I pulled the plug.\n[339.3s - 340.6s] I read every single message\n[341.8s - 345.1s] just to see if the agent leaked my private life.\n[346.8s - 348.0s] Nothing happened.\n[348.6s - 349.7s] But it could have.\n[351.0s - 353.8s] But there was the moment where it went viral.\n[354.7s - 357.3s] Today, the project is called OpenClaw.\n[357.3s - 362.5s] It's pretty much the fastest-growing open-source project.\n[363.6s - 365.1s] Its mascot is a lobster.\n[366.2s - 367.9s] It claws into your machine.\n[369.8s - 371.6s] Chenxin Huang calls it\n[371.6s - 374.8s] the operating system for personal AI.\n[375.6s - 378.3s] But by far, my favorite quote is from a friend\n[378.3s - 380.1s] who looked at this statistic and said,\n[380.7s - 383.3s] Peter, this is not hockey stick growth.\n[384.0s - 385.5s] This is stripper pole.\n[393.8s - 397.9s] And I was not ready.\n[398.6s - 400.4s] You know, when something blows up like this,\n[401.6s - 402.4s] everything explodes.\n[403.5s - 404.3s] Hundreds of messages.\n[405.5s - 407.0s] Reporters calling me in the middle of the night.\n[408.3s - 409.4s] Security vulnerabilities.\n[410.4s - 415.0s] And then the AI company,\n[415.0s - 417.3s] whose model most of my users loved,\n[418.1s - 419.1s] sent me a trademark claim.\n[419.6s - 422.1s] I had to rename the whole thing while it was taking off.\n[424.1s - 429.4s] You know, they even tried to push me away from the lobster.\n[431.7s - 433.5s] Like I was staring at the message.\n[433.6s - 435.7s] I was like, it's not even the same animal.\n[435.7s - 442.2s] And then they also cut off the model most of my users loved.\n[443.6s - 445.3s] You know, first the name.\n[447.0s - 447.9s] Then the lobster.\n[449.5s - 450.5s] Then the model.\n[452.0s - 454.9s] I was that close of just deleting the whole thing.\n[456.2s - 460.9s] But then I learned what people are building with it.\n[462.1s - 465.0s] So at ClawCon in Vienna,\n[465.3s - 467.2s] because yes, we have conferences already,\n[469.3s - 471.4s] and people wear lobster headbands,\n[472.5s - 477.9s] I met Stefan and his 60-year-old dad Gerhard,\n[478.7s - 483.2s] a beer sommelier who never wrote a single line of code.\n[484.3s - 487.6s] They connected OpenClaw via Bluetooth,\n[488.1s - 489.4s] sent it one prompt,\n[489.9s - 492.8s] and the agent did the whole 90-minute brew.\n[493.6s - 496.3s] Temperature ramps, hop additions, everything.\n[497.1s - 498.6s] And then they were like,\n[499.4s - 500.7s] what are we doing with all this beer?\n[501.8s - 504.4s] And the agent was, let's make a website.\n[505.3s - 508.1s] So they built a website, and then they added payments,\n[508.2s - 509.4s] and now they have a real product.\n[511.1s - 514.8s] And almost all of it was just done via the phone.\n[514.8s - 523.8s] In China, installing OpenClaw is called raising lobsters.\n[527.9s - 531.2s] were lining up at the Tencent office in Shenzhen\n[531.2s - 532.9s] to get their lobster installed.\n[534.7s - 536.4s] Shenzhen even gives out subsidies\n[536.4s - 539.9s] for people running businesses on OpenClaw.\n[541.4s - 547.0s] Now, if you install OpenClaw on your work machine,\n[548.2s - 549.4s] at least with the default settings,\n[549.9s - 550.8s] you might get fired.\n[552.6s - 556.1s] And then I met an entrepreneur in China\n[556.1s - 557.8s] who showed me a spreadsheet.\n[559.3s - 561.7s] Every employee, every day,\n[562.2s - 565.0s] one task automated by OpenClaw.\n[565.5s - 567.9s] If you miss too many days, you're fired.\n[569.0s - 572.3s] So, fired for using it.\n[573.3s - 575.0s] Fired for not using it.\n[577.3s - 578.1s] After Marrakesh,\n[579.7s - 581.8s] I thought, this is incredible.\n[582.3s - 583.7s] And it's also a little bit scary.\n[585.3s - 587.2s] How can we make it more scary?\n[589.1s - 592.5s] So I added a new feature, a heartbeat.\n[593.2s - 594.4s] You know, by default,\n[594.8s - 597.3s] the agent would only wake up when you send it a prompt.\n[598.2s - 598.8s] With the heartbeat,\n[599.7s - 602.0s] the agent would just wake up periodically,\n[602.9s - 605.3s] check your emails, check your calendar,\n[606.0s - 607.3s] follow up on loose ends.\n[607.7s - 609.8s] My initial prompt was simply,\n[610.7s - 611.6s] surprise me.\n[612.1s - 613.7s] And yes, that's true.\n[613.7s - 615.4s] That's kind of as scary as it sounds.\n[616.8s - 620.2s] So no large company would ship something like that.\n[620.8s - 623.6s] But I'm a random builder from Austria.\n[623.8s - 625.5s] I don't have a legal department.\n[628.4s - 633.9s] I built this sandbox, this sandcastle for me,\n[634.1s - 635.2s] and I made it open source,\n[635.6s - 637.1s] so other people could play with it\n[637.1s - 640.6s] and other people could raise their imagination.\n[640.6s - 645.4s] Imagine putting an agent into a meeting.\n[645.8s - 648.1s] Not for notes, we figured that out.\n[648.9s - 654.1s] A bidirectional model that can listen and hear at the same time.\n[655.4s - 656.9s] Somebody mentions a statistic,\n[657.2s - 659.7s] a sub-agent can spin off and check it for you.\n[660.2s - 661.3s] A decision is made,\n[662.0s - 665.8s] the agent can send a follow-up before the meeting even ends.\n[666.8s - 667.4s] In the future,\n[668.7s - 670.9s] we're not just going to have one agent.\n[671.6s - 673.5s] You might have your work agent,\n[674.3s - 675.3s] your personal claw,\n[675.8s - 676.7s] maybe one for health,\n[677.1s - 678.4s] maybe one for a relationship.\n[678.9s - 682.8s] And they all should work together in a secure way,\n[683.8s - 686.2s] because how did humanity level up?\n[687.7s - 690.3s] By specializing and collaborating.\n[690.7s - 693.4s] And agents are about to do the same.\n[694.2s - 697.0s] Imagine a company, a small business,\n[697.9s - 701.0s] that has 10 agents that are all specialized,\n[701.6s - 703.9s] taking over various parts of the business.\n[704.5s - 707.2s] We don't even have a name for what it might become,\n[707.3s - 708.4s] but we're about to find out.\n[710.0s - 714.5s] So I created the Open Claw Foundation,\n[715.1s - 718.1s] a non-profit, open source, forever.\n[719.0s - 722.3s] Because what Open Claw did for many people\n[722.3s - 728.7s] was it moved AI from this scary, nebulous thing\n[728.7s - 733.5s] into something that is fun and useful\n[734.1s - 735.1s] and maybe a bit weird.\n[735.7s - 739.4s] You know, lobsters and headbands and beer businesses.\n[740.9s - 744.3s] Because what we need in the future is more people\n[744.4s - 747.3s] spending more time with AI\n[747.3s - 751.2s] to better understand how powerful and transformative\n[751.2s - 753.0s] this technology really is.\n[754.5s - 755.1s] In New York,\n[757.9s - 758.9s] at Claw Con,\n[761.5s - 763.2s] yes, they're everywhere now,\n[764.4s - 766.0s] there were thousands of people\n[766.6s - 770.6s] that were discussing what their lobster did this week.\n[772.1s - 776.7s] A retiree in Shenzhen who automated their groceries.\n[777.5s - 780.2s] A teenager in Sao Paolo\n[780.4s - 783.4s] who built a tutoring business on Open Claw.\n[785.0s - 786.7s] Gerhard and his beer machine.\n[788.3s - 789.3s] None of them are programmers.\n[790.9s - 793.1s] All of them are builders.\n[793.7s - 796.2s] Because that's the real transformation.\n[797.0s - 800.1s] It's not the technology, it's the access.\n[800.6s - 803.5s] Agents change who can build things,\n[803.8s - 805.9s] and that door is not closing again.\n[806.5s - 811.1s] Because when you can prompt a prototype\n[811.1s - 814.4s] into existence in one hour,\n[815.4s - 816.3s] anything is possible.\n[816.9s - 820.1s] The next breakthrough can come from anyone,\n[821.5s - 822.1s] any country,\n[823.4s - 823.9s] any cafe.\n[825.7s - 829.7s] When even a burned-out founder\n[830.5s - 832.6s] staring at the screen,\n[835.1s - 837.0s] wondering if his spark is gone,\n[838.0s - 839.2s] can do something like that.\n[841.5s - 842.4s] It's not gone.\n[843.3s - 844.0s] It's just waiting.\n[845.4s - 846.7s] The lobster is loose,\n[847.2s - 849.4s] and it's not going back into the tank.\n[850.5s - 851.6s] Thank you very much.\n[860.7s - 862.0s] Come here.\n[863.1s - 864.8s] I mean,\n[866.5s - 870.7s] I think I want to say something personal to you,\n[870.8s - 872.8s] with love, but truth.\n[873.4s - 875.7s] You really terrify me.\n[877.2s - 878.9s] No, I'm serious.\n[879.0s - 881.6s] If Hollywood was to ever make a movie\n[881.6s - 881.6s] \n[881.6s - 881.6s] \n[881.6s - 881.6s] \n[881.6s - 881.6s] \n[881.6s - 885.0s] in which humanity opened Pandora's box\n[885.0s - 886.5s] and everything went crazy,\n[887.0s - 889.9s] you seriously could be cast as the star character.\n[891.7s - 892.3s] Yeah.\n[892.6s - 894.2s] Because the story we're told\n[894.2s - 898.0s] is that AI researchers are doing all this great stuff,\n[898.3s - 900.7s] but they're taking all these great efforts\n[900.7s - 903.6s] to ensure safety and make sure nothing bad could happen.\n[904.7s - 908.9s] You take glee in seeing what might happen\n[908.9s - 910.3s] if you just put it out there.\n[910.3s - 915.7s] Like, is any part of you feeling that that's a little bit reckless?\n[917.1s - 918.9s] I wouldn't say so.\n[919.4s - 920.9s] I see my work as a ...\n[922.2s - 924.1s] I see it as a window into the future.\n[925.9s - 927.9s] Like, in the very beginning,\n[928.8s - 930.4s] there were all these scary moments.\n[931.5s - 933.5s] Now we have proper security layers.\n[933.9s - 935.1s] You can have your sandbox,\n[935.1s - 940.8s] you can put your lobster into a very small, tiny box\n[940.8s - 942.7s] and really control what it can do.\n[943.5s - 945.7s] There are still some issues that we need to figure out,\n[946.4s - 949.2s] but the fact that so many people want this now\n[949.5s - 951.8s] will help to figure this out much faster.\n[952.6s - 954.8s] So I'm glad you mentioned the security layers.\n[956.3s - 962.1s] I mean, you're making a huge bet on human ingenuity using this.\n[962.1s - 965.1s] It's an incredible tool that suddenly can maximize\n[