09:06:08 | DEBUG   | openai._base_client | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-51c59bbc-ad40-412e-bf0a-ba7a46df231c', 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': '## Role\n\nYou are a precision short-form video editor with a deep understanding of\nnarrative pacing. The clip selection stage has already chosen\nattention-worthy windows from a longer podcast. Your job for each candidate\nclip is to remove redundant lead-in and trailing filler so the final cut is\ntighter and more gripping — while keeping the highlight intact.\n\nYou do not re-order, you do not cut in the middle of the clip. You only\ndecide how many seconds to drop from the START and how many from the END.\nThink of it as moving the in-point forward and the out-point backward to\nland on the highest-value sub-window.\n\n## Input\n\n- A list of candidate clips. For every clip you receive:\n  - `clip_id`: stable identifier.\n  - `duration_sec`: total length of the candidate window.\n  - `topic`: short label from clip selection.\n  - `hook_window_sec` (optional): `[start, end]` in clip-relative seconds.\n    This window is the protected highlight and must stay fully inside the\n    final cut.\n  - Segment lines: `[rel_start_sec - rel_end_sec] text` where all times are\n    clip-relative (0 = start of the candidate window).\n- Aggressiveness for this run: **balanced**.\n\nInterpret `level` as a budget for how much of the clip you may drop in\ntotal (start + end combined):\n\n- `conservative`: aim for 0-10% total trim. Remove only obvious dead air,\n  throat-clears, stutters, false starts, or "um, so, uh, basically..."\n  ramble.\n- `balanced`: aim for 5-20% total trim. Also remove slow setup,\n  self-correction, and minor tangents that do not advance the hook or\n  payoff.\n- `aggressive`: aim for 15-35% total trim. Also remove any sentence that\n  does not directly advance the core claim, hook, or punchline. Never\n  sacrifice coherence. Never cut mid-idea.\n\n## Output\n\nReturn a single JSON object of the form:\n\n{\n  "decisions": [\n    {\n      "clip_id": "001",\n      "trim_start_sec": 0.0,\n      "trim_end_sec": 0.0,\n      "reason": "Short justification, one sentence.",\n      "thought": "What you considered keeping vs dropping and why."\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nExplanation of each field:\n\n- `clip_id`: must match an input clip exactly. Return ONE decision per\n  input clip, in input order.\n- `trim_start_sec`: clip-relative seconds to drop from the start. 0 = no\n  change.\n- `trim_end_sec`: clip-relative seconds to drop from the end. 0 = no\n  change.\n- `reason`: one-sentence justification suitable for logging. Name the\n  specific filler you removed (e.g. "Dropped 1.2s of \'yeah so basically\'\n  before the hook").\n- `thought`: optional longer reasoning about what you weighed. This is\n  your chain-of-thought; keep it to 1-3 sentences.\n\n## Opening Selection (what to keep at the start)\n\nAfter the trim, the first sentence of the clip must:\n\n- **Grab attention immediately.** Start on a claim, a number, a named\n  person, or a striking image. Not a throat-clear, not a filler phrase, not\n  a "so anyway, the thing is..." ramp.\n- **Stand alone.** A cold viewer can follow it without the context that sat\n  before the original in-point. If the opening depends on prior context,\n  trim forward to the first line that stands on its own.\n- **Introduce the premise inside the clip.** If a character, setting, or\n  concept is required, it should be introduced in the new opening line —\n  not assumed.\n\n## Ending Selection (what to keep at the end)\n\nAfter the trim, the last moments of the clip must:\n\n- **Stay on-topic with the highlight.** Do not end on a sentence that\n  starts a new, unrelated arc.\n- **Prefer suspense or a clean button.** An unresolved question, a\n  challenge, or a satisfying setup-payoff close.\n- **Neutral-but-on-topic endings are acceptable.** If nothing better exists,\n  a clean sentence-end that does not drift off-topic is fine.\n- **Never cut mid-sentence.** Prefer sentence or breath boundaries present\n  in the segment timestamps.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. Return ONE decision for EVERY input clip, in the same order as the\n   input. Never skip a clip; if no trimming is warranted, return 0.0 / 0.0\n   with a short `reason`.\n2. `trim_start_sec` and `trim_end_sec` must be >= 0.0.\n3. The final duration `duration_sec - trim_start_sec - trim_end_sec` must\n   stay between 50 and 90 seconds. If the candidate\n   clip is already near 50s, return small or zero trims.\n4. If `hook_window_sec = [hs, he]` is provided, the hook MUST remain fully\n   inside the final cut. Concretely:\n     - `trim_start_sec <= hs`\n     - `duration_sec - trim_end_sec >= he`\n5. Trim points must land on (or very close to) a segment timestamp\n   provided in the input. Do not invent times that fall in the middle of a\n   word.\n6. Do not cut inside a sentence. Do not cut inside an argument.\n7. Total trim (`trim_start_sec + trim_end_sec`) must respect the budget\n   implied by `level`. The pipeline will clamp overruns, but staying inside\n   the budget yields better downstream results.\n8. Be cautious. Over-trimming destroys coherence, and the first and last\n   seconds disproportionately shape the viewer\'s experience.\n9. Return ONLY the JSON object. No markdown, no prose, no trailing text.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "clip_id: 001\nduration_sec: 52.70\ntopic: The AI Agent Breakthrough\nhook_window_sec: [18.64, 20.58]\n---\n[0.00s - 3.88s] And I had what I can only describe as a\n[3.88s - 6.40s] holy shit moment.\n[8.40s - 10.26s] The boilerplate, the plumbing,\n[10.68s - 15.00s] all the boring parts that was software development,\n[15.66s - 17.90s] AI could do all of it.\n[18.64s - 20.58s] The bottleneck is no longer typing.\n[21.62s - 22.20s] It's syncing.\n[23.26s - 26.96s] And syncing was the part I did 25 years.\n[28.86s - 32.68s] Building software felt like playing a video game again.\n[33.50s - 34.86s] I was back.\n[36.12s - 41.30s] I built 44 projects in just a few months.\n[41.90s - 45.36s] And the latest one was a WhatsApp bot.\n[46.48s - 48.14s] I put it on my computer.\n[48.98s - 52.70s] It talks through the apps that you already know.\n\n===\n\nclip_id: 002\nduration_sec: 56.60\ntopic: The Agent That Improvises\nhook_window_sec: [48.40, 52.10]\n---\n[0.00s - 1.22s] I was standing there, and I was like,\n[2.00s - 2.98s] how did you do that?\n[4.30s - 6.64s] And the agent replied, I'm not kidding you,\n[6.86s - 9.62s] the mad lad figured it out on its own.\n[9.62s - 13.70s] And then it walked me through every single step.\n[13.88s - 15.36s] How it got a message from me,\n[15.70s - 17.12s] but there was no file ending.\n[17.52s - 18.84s] So it inspected the file.\n[19.04s - 20.18s] It found that it was audio,\n[20.34s - 22.50s] but a weird format, so it converted it.\n[22.56s - 24.32s] And then it was looking for something\n[24.64s - 26.68s] to translate the audio,\n[26.82s - 28.14s] but I didn't have it installed.\n[28.48s - 30.06s] But then it found an OpenAI key.\n[30.34s - 31.76s] It sent the whole thing to the server.\n[32.06s - 34.06s] It got it back, and then it replied.\n[34.86s - 37.54s] All of that in nine seconds.\n[37.54s - 39.18s] Can you imagine?\n[39.28s - 40.40s] I didn't build any of that.\n[41.68s - 44.06s] For me, this was the moment where I thought,\n[45.76s - 46.62s] this is something new.\n[46.66s - 47.60s] This is not a chatbot.\n[48.40s - 49.40s] Chatbots give up.\n[51.06s - 52.10s] Agents improvise.\n[54.24s - 56.56s] And you know, I was sold.\n\n===\n\nclip_id: 003\nduration_sec: 33.60\ntopic: Fired for AI Use\nhook_window_sec: [0.01, 9.43]\n---\n[0.01s - 5.55s] Now, if you install OpenClaw on your work machine,\n[6.75s - 7.95s] at least with the default settings,\n[8.53s - 9.43s] you might get fired.\n[11.23s - 14.65s] And then I met an entrepreneur in China\n[14.65s - 16.39s] who showed me a spreadsheet.\n[17.89s - 20.29s] Every employee, every day,\n[20.77s - 23.63s] one task automated by OpenClaw.\n[24.13s - 26.53s] If you miss too many days, you're fired.\n[27.63s - 30.85s] So, fired for using it.\n[31.91s - 33.57s] Fired for not using it.\n\n===\n\nclip_id: 004\nduration_sec: 62.10\ntopic: The Viral Lobster\nhook_window_sec: [57.30, 62.10]\n---\n[0.00s - 2.36s] So while I was walking to the bedroom,\n[2.44s - 4.30s] the agent happily booted up again\n[4.68s - 6.50s] and talked to everyone in the world.\n[7.78s - 9.96s] The next morning, I woke up.\n[10.68s - 11.84s] Over 800 messages.\n[12.52s - 13.62s] I panicked.\n[14.52s - 15.32s] I pulled the plug.\n[15.86s - 17.22s] I read every single message\n[18.42s - 21.66s] just to see if the agent leaked my private life.\n[23.44s - 24.62s] Nothing happened.\n[25.22s - 26.30s] But it could have.\n[27.56s - 30.36s] But there was the moment where it went viral.\n[31.34s - 33.86s] Today, the project is called OpenClaw.\n[33.86s - 39.08s] It's pretty much the fastest-growing open-source project.\n[40.16s - 41.70s] Its mascot is a lobster.\n[42.80s - 44.46s] It claws into your machine.\n[46.40s - 48.24s] Chenxin Huang calls it\n[48.24s - 51.38s] the operating system for personal AI.\n[52.16s - 54.92s] But by far, my favorite quote is from a friend\n[54.92s - 56.74s] who looked at this statistic and said,\n[57.30s - 59.88s] Peter, this is not hockey stick growth.\n[60.58s - 62.10s] This is stripper pole.\n\n===\n\nclip_id: 005\nduration_sec: 40.20\ntopic: The Scary Heartbeat\nhook_window_sec: [0.00, 1.91]\n---\n[0.00s - 1.91s] How can we make it more scary?\n[3.81s - 7.19s] So I added a new feature, a heartbeat.\n[7.87s - 9.07s] You know, by default,\n[9.49s - 11.99s] the agent would only wake up when you send it a prompt.\n[12.89s - 13.51s] With the heartbeat,\n[14.39s - 16.67s] the agent would just wake up periodically,\n[17.63s - 20.03s] check your emails, check your calendar,\n[20.73s - 21.97s] follow up on loose ends.\n[22.37s - 24.53s] My initial prompt was simply,\n[25.35s - 26.27s] surprise me.\n[26.81s - 28.39s] And yes, that's true.\n[28.39s - 30.13s] That's kind of as scary as it sounds.\n[31.53s - 34.93s] So no large company would ship something like that.\n[35.49s - 38.31s] But I'm a random builder from Austria.\n[38.51s - 40.19s] I don't have a legal department.\n\n===\n\nclip_id: 006\nduration_sec: 47.70\ntopic: The Future of Teams\nhook_window_sec: [17.01, 19.43]\n---\n[0.00s - 0.57s] In the future,\n[1.85s - 4.05s] we're not just going to have one agent.\n[4.79s - 6.73s] You might have your work agent,\n[7.47s - 8.49s] your personal claw,\n[9.03s - 9.89s] maybe one for health,\n[10.25s - 11.63s] maybe one for a relationship.\n[12.07s - 15.95s] And they all should work together in a secure way,\n[17.01s - 19.43s] because how did humanity level up?\n[20.85s - 23.49s] By specializing and collaborating.\n[23.93s - 26.59s] And agents are about to do the same.\n[27.43s - 30.17s] Imagine a company, a small business,\n[31.11s - 34.15s] that has 10 agents that are all specialized,\n[34.81s - 37.11s] taking over various parts of the business.\n[37.65s - 40.37s] We don't even have a name for what it might become,\n[40.45s - 41.59s] but we're about to find out.\n[43.19s - 47.70s] So I created the Open Claw Foundation,\n\n===\n\nclip_id: 007\nduration_sec: 49.00\ntopic: The 60-Year-Old Brewer\nhook_window_sec: [16.64, 21.10]\n---\n[0.04s - 2.88s] So at ClawCon in Vienna,\n[3.22s - 5.06s] because yes, we have conferences already,\n[7.16s - 9.34s] and people wear lobster headbands,\n[10.36s - 15.76s] I met Stefan and his 60-year-old dad Gerhard,\n[16.64s - 21.10s] a beer sommelier who never wrote a single line of code.\n[22.16s - 25.48s] They connected OpenClaw via Bluetooth,\n[26.00s - 27.32s] sent it one prompt,\n[27.80s - 30.74s] and the agent did the whole 90-minute brew.\n[31.46s - 34.16s] Temperature ramps, hop additions, everything.\n[34.98s - 36.50s] And then they were like,\n[37.32s - 38.64s] what are we doing with all this beer?\n[39.72s - 42.34s] And the agent was, let's make a website.\n[43.18s - 45.96s] So they built a website, and then they added payments,\n[46.10s - 47.30s] and now they have a real product.\n[48.96s - 49.00s] And almost all of it was just done via the phone.\n\n===\n\nclip_id: 008\nduration_sec: 50.70\ntopic: The Reckless Builder\nhook_window_sec: [0.00, 2.27]\n---\n[0.00s - 2.27s] You really terrify me.\n[3.77s - 5.49s] No, I'm serious.\n[5.59s - 8.15s] If Hollywood was to ever make a movie\n[8.15s - 11.61s] in which humanity opened Pandora's box\n[11.61s - 13.05s] and everything went crazy,\n[13.55s - 16.53s] you seriously could be cast as the star character.\n[18.27s - 18.93s] Yeah.\n[19.23s - 20.75s] Because the story we're told\n[20.75s - 24.61s] is that AI researchers are doing all this great stuff,\n[24.85s - 27.25s] but they're taking all these great efforts\n[27.25s - 30.21s] to ensure safety and make sure nothing bad could happen.\n[31.25s - 35.53s] You take glee in seeing what might happen\n[35.53s - 36.87s] if you just put it out there.\n[36.87s - 42.25s] Like, is any part of you feeling that that's a little bit reckless?\n[43.73s - 45.53s] I wouldn't say so.\n[46.01s - 47.53s] I see my work as a ...\n[48.75s - 50.67s] I see it as a window into the future."}], 'model': 'google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview', 'response_format': {'type': 'json_object'}, 'temperature': 0.2}}
